# Caddy `forward_auth` wiring for the ac_auth sidecar.
#
# CURRENT REALITY: enforcement for the dashboard's own control routes lives in
# the Next.js middleware (web/src/middleware.ts), which calls the sidecar's
# GET /auth/verify itself. This Caddy block is the *edge* alternative — use it
# when a separate Caddy reverse proxy (e.g. on another host) needs to gate
# mutating routes before they reach an upstream that doesn't do its own check.
#
# The sidecar (ac-organic-lab-auth.service) listens on the Tailnet interface at
#   100.64.254.6:8009        <-- NOT loopback; 127.0.0.1:8009 is refused.
# A remote Caddy reaches it over the Tailnet at that address.
#
# GET /auth/verify returns:
#   200 + headers  X-Auth-User: <email>   X-Auth-Role: <role>   when authenticated
#   401                                                          otherwise
# Identity is resolved from EITHER the session cookie `ac_auth_session`
# (humans; overridable via AUTH_COOKIE_NAME) OR the `X-Api-Key` request header
# (machine principals). It does NOT use X-Forwarded-For. Caddy's forward_auth
# forwards the inbound request headers (incl. Cookie) to the sub-request by
# default, so both credentials reach the sidecar without extra config.
#
# Add the block below INSIDE the site block whose upstream you want to guard.
# forward_auth treats 2xx as allow and returns anything else (the 401) to the
# client; copy_headers injects the verified identity into the proxied request.
#
#   @control {
#       path /api/equipment/*/control/*
#       method POST PUT PATCH DELETE
#   }
#   forward_auth @control 100.64.254.6:8009 {
#       uri /auth/verify
#       copy_headers X-Auth-User X-Auth-Role
#   }
#
# forward_auth only guards the matched mutating routes; reads stay open. The
# action-name + kind bypasses (lights, camera, env) live in the app / Next.js
# middleware, not here — Caddy handles the broad path/method surface only.
